{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-g1-reviewer-fable-32","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:30108f0d34292b11cab8","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"Within the supplied files of case:30108f0d34292b11cab8, the workflow enters all 20 weekly rows (four repeated rows per each of the 5 digesters named by the independent-unit column digester_id) as independent Bernoulli trials into an exact binomial test, and the report's selected result (15/20 wins, p = 0.0414 < 0.05) depends on this repeated per-unit entry: collapsing to one entry per independent digester (4 of 5 digesters with majority pretreatment wins) yields an exact two-sided binomial p = 0.375, so the significance claim is not supported at the independent-unit level.","root_cause":"The analysis script tallies a win/loss verdict for every weekly session row and passes wins=15, n=20 to scipy.stats.binomtest, a procedure whose validity assumes each of the n trials is independent. Because each digester contributes four rows and the data description states both that digester_id is the independent unit and that readings from the same digester move together (confirmed empirically by the report's own breakdown: D-01 4/4 wins, D-02 4/4 wins, D-05 1/4 wins), the effective sample size is 5 units, not 20 trials; the inflated n manufactures the sub-0.05 p-value that the report reads as evidence for the pretreatment effect.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description designates digester_id as the independent-unit column and states each digester contributes four rows, so rows within a digester are repeated entries of the same independent unit.","observed_value":"Independent unit = digester_id; 4 rows per digester, 5 digesters, 20 rows total","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":14,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"Independent unit column: digester_id"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":18,"end_line":18,"quoted_text":"- digester_id: label of the digester the session belongs to (D-01 through D-05); the same digester contributes four rows, one per study week"}]},{"description":"The data description explicitly warns of within-digester dependence across weeks, so the four rows per digester cannot be treated as independent trials.","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":24,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"Digesters differ from one another in sludge blend, mixing regime and retention\ntime, so the weekly readings taken from one digester tend to move together."}]},{"description":"The workflow counts one win per row across all sessions and then feeds the row-level tally into scipy.stats.binomtest with n equal to the number of rows (20), a procedure that assumes each entered trial is independent.","observed_value":"binomtest(wins=15, n=20, p=0.5, two-sided)","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":30,"end_line":34,"quoted_text":"    for row in sessions:\n        pretreated = float(row[\"pretreated_yield_m3_per_kgvs\"])\n        control = float(row[\"control_yield_m3_per_kgvs\"])\n        won = 1 if pretreated > control else 0\n        wins += won"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":89,"end_line":90,"quoted_text":"    n_sessions = len(sessions)\n    result = binomtest(wins, n_sessions, NULL_WIN_RATE, alternative=\"two-sided\")"}]},{"description":"The report explicitly declares every row an independent Bernoulli trial and bases its statistical conclusion on the 20-row binomial test.","observed_value":"p = 0.0414 over 20 row-level trials","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":23,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"Every logged session is treated as one independent Bernoulli trial and the\nwin tally is tested against a fair-coin null of 0.5 with the exact two-sided\nbinomial test `scipy.stats.binomtest`."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":30,"end_line":30,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Exact two-sided binomial test (scipy.stats.binomtest) over 20 weekly sampling sessions: the pretreated train won 15 of 20 sessions, proportion 0.750, p = 0.0414, which is below 0.05, so the log is read as evidence that thermal-hydrolysis pretreatment raises specific methane yield."}]},{"description":"The report's own per-digester breakdown shows strong within-unit clustering of outcomes (two digesters win every session, one digester loses three of four), confirming the rows from one digester are not exchangeable independent trials; collapsing to the 5 independent digesters (4 of 5 with majority wins) gives an exact two-sided binomial p of 0.375, so the p<0.05 result exists only through the repeated per-unit entries.","observed_value":"Per-digester wins out of 4: D-01=4, D-02=4, D-03=3, D-04=3, D-05=1; unit-level test binomtest(4,5,0.5) two-sided p = 0.375","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":15,"end_line":19,"quoted_text":"| D-01 | 4 | 4 |\n| D-02 | 4 | 4 |\n| D-03 | 4 | 3 |\n| D-04 | 4 | 3 |\n| D-05 | 4 | 1 |"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":18,"end_line":21,"quoted_text":"D-05,1,night,0.344,0.359\nD-05,2,day,0.352,0.371\nD-05,3,day,0.361,0.348\nD-05,4,night,0.339,0.366"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description itself states 'One trial is: one row', which could be read as authorizing row-level trials; however, this describes what a logged row records, and the same description's independent-unit designation (digester_id) and the explicit within-digester correlation warning make clear that rows are not independent for inference, so this does not license entering each row as an independent trial in a binomial test.","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":13,"end_line":15,"quoted_text":"One row is: one weekly paired sampling session on a single digester, holding that week's pretreated and control specific methane yields side by side\nIndependent unit column: digester_id\nOne trial is: one row"}]},{"description":"The report transparently discloses the distinct-digester count and the per-digester win breakdown, so the repeated entry of each digester is not concealed; transparency, however, does not remove the dependence violation in the selected binomial result, which still uses n=20 row-level trials.","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":9,"end_line":11,"quoted_text":"- Sessions read from `data/input.csv`: 20\n- Distinct digester identifiers in the file: 5\n- Sessions won by the pretreated train: 15"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I tried to defeat the finding in three ways. (1) I checked whether the workflow deduplicates or aggregates to one entry per digester before testing: it does not — tally_wins accumulates one win/loss per row and main calls binomtest(wins, n_sessions, ...) with n_sessions = len(sessions) = 20 rows, and the per-digester breakdown is used only for a display table, never for inference. (2) I checked whether the within-digester rows behave as if independent, which would make the repeated entry statistically harmless: they do not — outcomes cluster strongly by digester (D-01 and D-02 win 4/4, D-05 wins 1/4), consistent with the description's warning that a digester's weekly readings move together. (3) I checked whether the conclusion survives analysis at the declared independent-unit level: recomputing with one summary per digester, 4 of 5 digesters show a majority of pretreatment wins, and an exact two-sided binomial test on 4/5 gives p = 0.375 (even a session-count-weighted view cannot rescue p < 0.05 from 5 exchangeable units), so the selected p = 0.0414 conclusion depends entirely on treating the 20 correlated rows as independent trials. All three attempts failed to overturn the finding.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}